  "prompt": "Edit the FIRST reference image, a continuous blue-gray industrial metal corridor mother, into a corrected reusable PIXEL ART corridor. Use the SECOND reference only as the exact material, colors, bevel contrast, gold bolt and pixel-cluster style reference: this is the matching large floor it must connect to. Preserve the dark blue-gray scratched steel palette and crisp stepped square pixel marks. Do not add a different lighting treatment, realistic shading, global gradient, softer brush marks, vignette or new hue. Colors of the plate interiors and seam highlights should match the second reference.\n\nRender ONE continuous tall rectangular top-down metal surface, edge to edge, no background, no margins, no transparency, no montage or contact sheet. Keep EXACT portrait ratio 1:3. Think of this whole image as a 128 by 384 pixel mother that will later be cut into three 128 by 128 square tiles at one third and two thirds of the height. Do not outline or separate the three tiles.\n\nStructural correction:\n- The old side rails are too thin. Replace them with clearly visible solid connected metal beams spanning the FULL height on both left and right edges, EACH exactly 1/16 of total image width (8px at conceptual128). Same broad double bevel dark-blue metal rim and pale blue-grey highlight as the second reference. Keep the overall floor solid across its full width.\n- The very top and bottom ends have matching closed horizontal beams with gold bolts. Each is 8px tall in conceptual pixels. Square pixel corner joins connect to the full-width side beams; no chamfer, detached fragments, rounded edge or outside background.\n- The steel has TWO columns and SIX rows on a strict modular grid. Main interior vertical seam at half of the full image width. Five internal horizontal seams placed at exactly 1/6, 2/6, 3/6, 4/6 and 5/6 of TOTAL image height, corresponding to conceptual y=64,128,192,256,320. The tile cuts are y128 and y256. Beams lie INSIDE the original grid; do not inset and then divide the remaining interior into six equal rows. That mistaken inset subdivision was causing cumulative drift.\n- At one-third and two-thirds height a shared horizontal plate joint continues exactly through the cut. The top and bottom rims are only at the total mother endpoints; no crossbar endcap at the tile cuts.\n- Six plate rows have equal 64px pitch. Their scratches can vary in coherent coarse pixel clusters, but do not rotate, resize, stagger or curve the board grid.\n- 2px conceptual plate bevels, small matching golden square bolts at plate corners. Sharp nearest-neighbor looking clusters, no blur and no antialiasing.\n\nOutput only this one continuous 1:3 opaque corridor mother. It must look cut from the same industrial pixel floor as the second reference. No text, guides, labels, grid overlay, colored markers, shadows outside the material or extra assets.\n",
